<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiuquan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiuquan is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>. It was founded as a Han commandery in the late 2nd century BCE, and its name, meaning &quot;Wine Spring&quot;, comes from a legend about the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a>. In 405 it became the capital of the Western Liang state, and it remained an important station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. A satellite launch centre named after it lies in the desert to the north-east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiuquan.jpg" alt="Jiuquan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pcemzumark · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BC%93%E6%A5%BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
